      },
      "worked": "Intersections must satisfy $|x-2|+k=\\dfrac{x}{2}-2$ and $x\\geq0$.\n\nOn the left branch, $0\\leq x\\leq2$:\n$$\\begin{align*}2-x+k&=\\frac{x}{2}-2\\\\x&=\\frac{8+2k}{3}.\\end{align*}$$\nThis root lies in $[0,2]$ exactly when $-4\\leq k\\leq-1$.\n\nOn the right branch, $x\\geq2$:\n$$\\begin{align*}x-2+k&=\\frac{x}{2}-2\\\\x&=-2k.\\end{align*}$$\nThis root is valid exactly when $k\\leq-1$.\n\nFor $k<-4$, only the right-branch root is in the domain of $g$, giving one intersection. For $-4\\leq k<-1$, both roots are valid and distinct, giving two intersections. At $k=-1$, both roots coincide at $x=2$, giving one intersection. For $k>-1$, there are no intersections.\n\nTherefore, $k=-1$ or $k<-4$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
